The rigid-body layer of the non-relativistic classical mechanics collection,
all of it mathematics: the measure-theoretic inertia tensor of a rigid body, the
kinematics of a rigid placement, the torque-free Euler equations with their
nonlinear stability and instability statements, and the Euler-angle reduction of
the heavy symmetric top. Every item carries `domain: mathematics` and depends
only on mathematical suppliers; nothing here asserts that a physical body is
rigid, that its motion obeys any balance law, or that the parameters of the
heavy-top system are measured quantities. Those readings belong to the consuming
classical-mechanics pages, which add their own postulates, interpretations and
empirical qualifications.

**Kinematic foundations.** [[lem-cm-matrix-curve-derivative-rules]] proves the
sum, transpose and product rules for matrix-valued curves and the skew-symmetry
of $R^{\mathsf T}R'$ for a curve in $SO(3)$;
[[lem-cm-skew-matrix-cross-product-correspondence]] establishes the bijection
$[\omega]_\times z=\omega\times z$ between skew matrices and vectors and its
conjugation law $R[\omega]_\times R^{\mathsf T}=[R\omega]_\times$;
[[def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity]] then defines a rigid
placement $x(t,\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\rho$, its body and spatial angular velocities, and
its material velocities.

**Inertia.** [[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]] defines a mass
distribution as a bounded finite Borel measure on $\mathbb R^3$, with total mass,
first moment and inertia matrix
$I_{jk}=\int(\lVert\rho\rVert_2^2\delta_{jk}-\rho_j\rho_k)\,d\nu$;
[[lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite]] proves every displayed integral finite;
[[lem-cm-inertia-matrix-properties]] proves symmetry, the identity
$\omega^{\mathsf T}I\omega=\int\lVert\omega\times\rho\rVert_2^2\,d\nu$ and the
criterion that $I$ is positive definite exactly when no direction supports mass
on a line through the origin; and
[[cor-cm-principal-axes-of-the-inertia-matrix]] supplies the orthonormal
principal axes with positive principal moments in the nondegenerate case, using
the real spectral theorem.

**Dynamics.** [[thm-cm-rigid-kinetic-energy-and-angular-momentum]] computes
$K=\frac12m\lVert c'\rVert_2^2+\frac12\omega^{\mathsf T}I\omega$ and
$L_c=RI\omega$ for a centred distribution, by integrating the pointwise
identities of the placement. [[thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants]]
deduces Euler's equations $I\omega'+\omega\times I\omega=R^{\mathsf T}\tau$ from
an explicitly adopted torque balance, and in the torque-free case the equation
$l'=l\times(I^{-1}l)$ with the conserved quantities
$\lVert l\rVert_2^2=\sum_jl_j^2$ and $\sum_jl_j^2/(2I_j)$ and the component
equations in a principal basis.
[[thm-cm-stability-of-extreme-principal-axes]] proves Lyapunov stability of spin
about the largest and smallest principal axes by the invariant certificates
$C/(2I_1)-E$ and $E-C/(2I_3)$ together with compact continuation;
[[thm-cm-instability-of-the-intermediate-axis]] proves instability of the
intermediate axis by an explicit invariant branch on which
$l_1'\ge c_0l_1>0$, with a logarithmic escape-time bound; and
[[thm-cm-linearized-torque-free-euler-equations]] records the linearization at a
principal-axis spin and the oscillatory/exponential dichotomy, with all solution
formulas verified by differentiation and uniqueness.

**Euler angles and the heavy top.**
[[lem-cm-coordinate-rotation-matrices]] collects the coordinate rotation
matrices $R_z,R_y$, their composition law and their derivatives;
[[def-cm-euler-angle-parametrization-and-heavy-top-system]] defines the
Euler-angle parametrization $\Theta(\varphi,\theta,\psi)=R_z(\varphi)R_y(\theta)R_z(\psi)$
and the heavy-top system by its three displayed second-order equations;
[[lem-cm-euler-angle-angular-velocity]] computes the body angular velocity in
Euler angles, including $\omega_3=\psi'+\varphi'\cos\theta$ and
$\omega_1^2+\omega_2^2=\theta'^2+\varphi'^2\sin^2\theta$;
[[thm-cm-heavy-top-reduction]] derives the two conserved momenta, the explicit
rates $\varphi',\psi'$, the conserved reduced energy and the one-dimensional
equation $A\theta''=-U'(\theta)$.

The companion page [[cm-math-rigid-examples]] records finite arithmetic checks
of the inertia computations, the spin-stability certificates and the heavy-top
reduction.
